---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/PageLayout.astro";

// Version MM-PRIVACY-v1.0, in force from 2026-10-27. Every statement about the app is
// backed by the ModelMeter source (privacy_points in modelmeter-ui/src/viewmodel.rs,
// the Android manifest and build.gradle.kts). Change this page in the same release
// that changes what the app does.
const metadata = {
  title: "ModelMeter Privacy Notice - Aunova",
  description:
    "How the ModelMeter Android app handles data: the device check stays on your phone, the free version shows Google ads, and the remove-ads purchase goes through Google Play.",
};
---

<Layout title={metadata.title} description={metadata.description}>
  <section class="legal-page">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="legal-header">
        <h1 class="legal-title">ModelMeter Privacy Notice</h1>
        <p class="legal-updated">Last updated: October 27, 2026</p>
      </div>

      <div class="legal-content">
        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>About this notice</h2>
          <p class="legal-intro">
            This notice covers one thing: the <strong>ModelMeter</strong> app for Android
            (package <code>com.aunova.modelmeter</code>). ModelMeter checks which AI models your
            phone can run. This notice explains what happens to data when you use it. It is
            written by Aunova OÜ, which makes the app.
          </p>
          <p>
            It does not cover the rest of aunova.net, which has its own
            <a href="/en/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
          </p>
          <p>The short version:</p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>The device check runs entirely on your phone. Nothing it works out is sent anywhere.</li>
            <li>We have no account, no sign-in, no analytics and no crash reporting of our own.</li>
            <li>
              The free version shows a Google ad. Google's ad software collects data for that, and
              this notice says what.
            </li>
            <li>We, Aunova, receive nothing about you from the app.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Who is responsible, and how to reach us</h2>
          <p>The company responsible for ModelMeter is:</p>
          <p>
            <strong>Aunova OÜ, Ahtri tn 12, 15551 Tallinn, Harju maakond, Estonia (registry code 16837438, VAT: EE102670613)</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            You can reach us by email at
            <a href="mailto:chris@aunova.net">chris@aunova.net</a>, or by post at the address above.
          </p>
          <p>
            We have not appointed a Data Protection Officer, and we are not required to have one.
            Data protection is handled internally, and the email address above reaches the person
            responsible for it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>The device check stays on your phone</h2>
          <p>
            ModelMeter reads your phone's memory, free storage and hardware details, and compares
            them with a list of AI models. That list is built into the app.
          </p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>The check runs entirely on the phone. It works offline.</li>
            <li>What ModelMeter works out about your device is never sent anywhere, to us or to anyone else.</li>
            <li>ModelMeter's own code does not save any of it to the phone's storage.</li>
            <li>There is no account and no sign-in.</li>
            <li>There is no analytics and no crash reporting of our own.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Permissions</h2>
          <p>
            ModelMeter never asks you to grant a permission. It needs none of the ones Android
            prompts for: no location, no contacts, no files, no phone state.
          </p>
          <p>
            The app does declare two permissions that Android grants without asking. Both are there
            for Google's ad software, not for anything the check does:
          </p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li><strong>Internet</strong>, to fetch an ad and the consent form.</li>
            <li><strong>Advertising ID</strong>, because Google's ad software reads your phone's advertising ID.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Ads in the free version</h2>
          <p>
            The free version shows one banner ad, on the Models screen only. The ad is the one part
            of the app that uses the network on its own. It comes from Google AdMob, through the
            Google Mobile Ads SDK.
          </p>
          <p>
            Google states that its Mobile Ads SDK collects and shares the following data
            automatically, for advertising, analytics and fraud prevention:
          </p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>
              <strong>IP address</strong>, which may be used to estimate the general location of
              your device.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Product interactions</strong>, such as app launches, taps and video views.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Diagnostic information</strong> about how the app and the SDK perform, such
              as app launch time, hang rate and energy usage.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Device and account identifiers</strong>: the Android advertising ID, the app
              set ID and, where applicable, other identifiers related to accounts signed in on the
              device.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Google states that this data is encrypted in transit. The source is Google's own
            <a href="https://developers.google.com/admob/android/privacy/play-data-disclosure">data
            disclosure for the Mobile Ads SDK</a>, which Google keeps up to date.
          </p>
          <p>
            Nothing ModelMeter works out about your device is part of the ad request. We do not
            pass Google any data of our own.
          </p>
          <p>
            Google uses the data it collects through its SDK under its own rules, and for AdMob it
            acts as an independent controller. See
            <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy policy</a> and
            <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites">how Google uses
            information from sites or apps that use its services</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Consent for ads</h2>
          <p>
            Before the ad software starts, ModelMeter asks Google's User Messaging Platform whether
            consent is needed where you are. In the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom and
            Switzerland, and wherever else the rules require it, Google's consent form is shown
            first. No ad is requested until that question is settled. Your answer decides what the
            ad software may use, including whether ads may be personalised.
          </p>
          <p>
            The app does not have a menu to change that answer later. What you can do:
          </p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Reset or delete your advertising ID</strong> with the ad ID controls in your
              phone's Android settings. This works for every app on the phone, not just ModelMeter.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Clear ModelMeter's storage</strong> in Android settings, under Apps, then
              ModelMeter. That removes the consent answer saved on the phone, and where the rules
              require it you are asked again the next time the app starts the ad software.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Buy the remove-ads option</strong>, described below. When Google Play confirms,
              as the app opens, that the purchase is on your Google account, ModelMeter neither
              shows the consent form nor starts the ad software. If Google Play has not answered
              within about three seconds of the app opening, ModelMeter starts the ad software for
              that session anyway, so on such a start a buyer can still see the consent form and
              the ad.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>The remove-ads purchase</h2>
          <p>
            A one-time in-app purchase removes the ad. Nothing else changes: the check and the
            model list are the same either way. "Restore purchases" on the About screen brings the
            purchase to another device signed in to the same Google account.
          </p>
          <p>
            The purchase runs through Google Play Billing. Google handles the payment. We never see
            your card or any other payment details.
          </p>
          <p>
            The app reads from Google Play only what it needs to remove the ad: whether this
            Google account owns the purchase, and the purchase token Google uses to identify it.
            The app uses that token only to confirm the purchase to Google Play. It is not sent
            to us.
          </p>
          <p>
            Like every Google Play developer, we can see an order record for each sale in Google
            Play Console. Google decides what it contains. It includes an order number, the date,
            the product, the price and tax, and the buyer's country, and in some cases the region
            and postcode. We use it only for our accounts.
          </p>
          <p>
            See the <a href="https://play.google.com/about/play-terms/">Google Play Terms of
            Service</a> and <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy
            policy</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Links that leave the app</h2>
          <p>
            Some things in ModelMeter open a web address. Tapping one hands that address to your
            browser and leaves ModelMeter. From there, the site you visit and its own privacy
            policy apply. Nothing about your device or your check goes with the link.
          </p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>
              <strong>A model's page</strong> opens on Hugging Face. See the
              <a href="https://huggingface.co/privacy">Hugging Face privacy policy</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Tideproof</strong>, Aunova's other product, opens at tideproof.org. The link
              is tagged so that tideproof.org can tell the visit came from ModelMeter. See the
              <a href="https://tideproof.org/privacy/">Tideproof privacy page</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The banner ad</strong>, in the free version, opens whatever the advertiser's
              link points to. That link is not ours. What Google's ad software sends with the tap is
              covered by Google's policies above, and the advertiser's site has its own.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Our lawful basis</h2>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>
              <strong>The device check</strong> involves no processing of personal data by us. It
              runs on your phone and nothing leaves it.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Ads.</strong> Where the law requires consent, the ad software uses your data
              only as far as your answer in Google's consent form allows. GDPR Article 6(1)(a).
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The purchase.</strong> Handling the purchase status so that the ad is removed
              is necessary to give you what you paid for. Article 6(1)(b). Keeping order records for
              our accounts is a legal obligation. Article 6(1)(c).
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>If you email us</strong>, we use your message to answer it. Article 6(1)(f),
              our legitimate interest in replying to you.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>No decision is made about you automatically by us.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
          <p>
            From the app itself, we keep nothing, because we receive nothing. The two exceptions
            are not app data:
          </p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Order records</strong> stay in Google Play Console, and we keep them for as
              long as accounting law requires.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Emails you send us</strong> are kept until your question is settled, and then
              deleted.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            What Google keeps from its ad software and from Google Play is set by Google's policies,
            linked above.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Your rights</h2>
          <p>
            You have the following rights over any personal data we hold about you. Exercising any
            of them is free, and you never have to justify asking.
          </p>
          <ul class="legal-list">
            <li><strong>Access</strong> to the data and a copy of it. GDPR Article 15.</li>
            <li><strong>Rectification</strong> of inaccurate data. Article 16.</li>
            <li><strong>Erasure</strong> of your data. Article 17.</li>
            <li><strong>Restriction</strong> of what we do with it. Article 18.</li>
            <li><strong>Portability</strong> of the data you gave us. Article 20.</li>
            <li><strong>Objection</strong> to processing based on our legitimate interest. Article 21.</li>
            <li>
              <strong>Withdraw consent</strong> at any time, without affecting what was done before.
              Article 7(3). For ads, see <strong>Consent for ads</strong> above.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Complain</strong> to a supervisory authority, and seek a remedy in court. See
              below.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Because the app sends us nothing, we usually cannot link any data to you from ModelMeter
            alone. The data the ad software collects is held by Google. To exercise your rights
            over it, use Google's own tools, described in
            <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy policy</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            To ask us, email <a href="mailto:chris@aunova.net">chris@aunova.net</a> or write to the
            postal address above. You do not need to cite a law or fill in a form. We answer within
            30 calendar days. If a request is complex we may extend that by up to two further
            months, and we will tell you inside the first 30 days, and why.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Complaining to a supervisory authority</h2>
          <p>
            We would rather hear from you first, but you are not obliged to come to us at all. You
            can complain directly to our supervisory authority:
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Estonian Data Protection Inspectorate</strong>
            (<em>Andmekaitse Inspektsioon</em>), Tatari 39, 10134 Tallinn, Estonia. Telephone
            +372 627 4135. Website <a href="https://www.aki.ee">www.aki.ee</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            You may also complain to the data protection authority of the EU or EEA country where
            you live or work, and you may seek a remedy in court.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Children</h2>
          <p>ModelMeter is not directed at children.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Changes to this notice</h2>
          <p>
            We update this notice when what the app does changes, and the version and date change
            with it. If a new version of the app sends anything new off the phone, this notice
            changes first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="legal-section">
          <h2>Version and effective date</h2>
          <p>
            Version <strong>MM-PRIVACY-v1.0</strong>, in force from <strong>27 October 2026</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>This notice is pending review by qualified counsel. It is not legal advice.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .legal-page {
    padding: var(--space-3xl) 0 var(--space-5xl);
    min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-height));
  }

  .legal-header {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3xl);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .legal-title {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--color-primary), var(--color-secondary));
    background-size: 200% 200%;
    animation: gradient-shift 5s ease infinite;
  }

  .legal-updated {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .legal-content {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .legal-section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .legal-section h2 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .legal-intro {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .legal-section p {
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .legal-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: var(--space-md) 0;
  }

  .legal-list li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: var(--space-xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .legal-list li::before {
    content: "→";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--color-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .legal-list li strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .legal-section a {
    color: var(--color-primary);
    text-decoration: underline;
    transition: opacity var(--transition-fast);
  }

  .legal-section a:hover {
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  @keyframes gradient-shift {
    0%, 100% { background-position: 0% 50%; }
    50% { background-position: 100% 50%; }
  }

  /* Mobile styles */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .legal-page {
      padding: var(--space-2xl) 0 var(--space-3xl);
    }

    .legal-header {
      margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
      padding-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    }

    .legal-section {
      margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    }

    .legal-section h2 {
      font-size: 1.5rem;
    }

    .legal-intro {
      font-size: 1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
